From: Crescentm <guyues@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 15:09:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: clear recovery flag when starting a hw skipped by recovery

"wifi: ath12k: fix MLO station firmware crash recovery" moves the
clear_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY) from ath12k_core_reconfigure_on_crash()
to ath12k_mac_op_reconfig_complete(). That callback only runs for a hw
that mac80211 reconfigures after ieee80211_restart_hw(). If no interface
was up when the firmware crashed, ath12k_core_post_reconfigure_recovery()
skips the hw (state OFF) and ieee80211_reconfig() returns early because
open_count is 0, so reconfig_complete() never runs and the flag stays set
until the driver is reloaded.

With the flag stuck, every later scan fails with -EBUSY ("prevent scan
during firmware recovery"), keys for missing peers are silently ignored
and MLO link changes are rejected once an interface is brought up.

reconfig_complete() also finishes the reset bookkeeping of each device:
once all of its radios have recovered it drops reset_count, completes
reset_complete, clears is_reset and resets fail_cont_count. For a hw
that was OFF this never happens either, so is_reset stays true and
reset_count stays at 1. The next firmware crash then makes
ath12k_core_reset() wait ATH12K_RESET_TIMEOUT_HZ for a reset that is not
in progress and count the recovery as failed, on every later crash.

When a hw goes from OFF to ON in ath12k_mac_op_start(), do the same for
each radio's device whose firmware is up again (CRASH_FLUSH cleared):
clear the flag and, if the device is still marked as resetting, count
the radio as recovered and complete the reset once all radios of the
device have been counted, exactly as reconfig_complete() does. This runs
under the wiphy lock and ah->hw_mutex, like reconfig_complete(). Such a
hw took no part in the recovery, so there is no reconfiguration left for
the flag to protect.

Signed-off-by: Crescentm <guyues@outlook.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mac.c
@@ -9937,6 +9937,39 @@ int ath12k_mac_op_start(struct ieee80211_hw *hw)
 	switch (ah->state) {
 	case ATH12K_HW_STATE_OFF:
 		ah->state = ATH12K_HW_STATE_ON;
+
+		/* A hw that was not started when the firmware crashed is
+		 * skipped by the recovery: mac80211 does not reconfigure it and
+		 * ath12k_mac_op_reconfig_complete() is never called for it, so
+		 * ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY would stay set and the reset would never
+		 * be accounted as complete. Once the firmware is back up, do
+		 * what ath12k_mac_op_reconfig_complete() does for these radios.
+		 */
+		for_each_ar(ah, ar, i) {
+			struct ath12k_base *ab = ar->ab;
+			int recovery_count;
+
+			if (test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &ab->dev_flags))
+				continue;
+
+			clear_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY, &ab->dev_flags);
+
+			if (!ab->is_reset)
+				continue;
+
+			recovery_count = atomic_inc_return(&ab->recovery_count);
+
+			ath12k_dbg(ab, ATH12K_DBG_BOOT, "recovery count %d\n",
+				   recovery_count);
+
+			if (recovery_count == ab->num_radios) {
+				atomic_dec(&ab->reset_count);
+				complete(&ab->reset_complete);
+				ab->is_reset = false;
+				atomic_set(&ab->fail_cont_count, 0);
+				ath12k_dbg(ab, ATH12K_DBG_BOOT, "reset success\n");
+			}
+		}
 		break;
 	case ATH12K_HW_STATE_RESTARTING:
 		ah->state = ATH12K_HW_STATE_RESTARTED;
